            <ArticleTitle>Ambulatory systolic-diastolic pressure regression index predicts acute coronary syndromes.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>179-82</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3109/08037051.2012.759328</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Ambulatory systolic-diastolic pressure regression index (ASDPRI) is a composite marker of cardiovascular properties. The study was conducted to investigate the relationship between ASDPRI and cardiovascular events in patients with significant coronary atherosclerosis confirmed in angiography.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Associations between ASDPRI and cardiovascular events were examined in 891 subjects referred for diagnostic coronary angiography. ASDPRI was calculated as 1 minus the slope of the linear relation between 24-h DBP and 24-h SBP.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">During follow-up (median 6.7 years), 135 acute coronary syndromes (ACS), including five deaths and 55 strokes occurred in the studied group. There was a higher 24-h ASDPRI in patients with ACS than in patients with no ACS (0.35 ± 01 vs 0.30 ± 0.1, p &lt; 0.01); 24-h ASDPRI was the independent predictor for ACS (OR = 4.0; 95% CI 1.3-12.0).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">ASDPRI has an important role as a risk factor of ACS in patients with coronary atherosclerosis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
